{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a starter pack being purchased without any deliberate action on their part: no tap, and no fingerprint or password challenge despite their device settings requiring authentication for purchases. What is being described is a purchase flow that executed with no confirmation or friction, i.e., an accidental purchase. The closing sentence ('Not sure if it's just a glitch or what') is speculative uncertainty about the cause, not a definitive attribution of the behavior to a technical fault, so R11 does not suppress the label; the described design behavior is that a real-money purchase was completed without the player's input or an authentication barrier.",
    "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "it notified me that I bought a starter pack. I didn't tap anything and my settings are set up to where I have to input my fingerprint or password to make a purchase and it did not do that",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases [low-level] under M_EasyToPurchase",
      "rationale": "The span shows a real-money purchase executed with no player action and no confirmation or authentication barrier, matching the accidental purchase pattern; the reviewer's 'not sure if it's just a glitch' is speculation about cause rather than an affirmative bug attribution, so the described no-confirmation purchase design is still coded."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two separate design behaviours. First, a real-money purchase fired from a double-tap on screen, which the reviewer calls accidental: that implies the storefront committed the purchase without any meaningful confirmation or safeguard between the input and the charge. Second, after the reviewer obtained a refund through Google Play, the game locked the account and demanded repayment of the refunded amount before restoring access: that is a payment wall — the game is unplayable until money is paid. The customer-service tone of the complaint does not change the underlying mechanics described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "accidentally brought a pack when I double tapped the screen",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases indicator: 'Making it easy to accidentally spend money with no confirmation or ability to undo or refund the action.'",
      "rationale": "A single double-tap executed a paid pack purchase, implying the interface commits real-money transactions without requiring a deliberate confirmatory step."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "now my game has been locked and they are holding my account to ransom until I pay the equivalent of the refund I got",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall indicator: 'The game becomes impossible to continue playing without payment.'",
      "rationale": "The account is locked and continued access to the game is contingent on making a payment, which is an unplayable-without-payment wall rather than a bug or technical fault."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing Ludo King's dice rolls as rigged: they assert that the outcome of each match is decided in advance and the dice simply follow the game's predetermined script, giving one player repeated 6s and the other low rolls. The complaint is not about bad luck in a fair game but about the game secretly controlling the results to pick a winner. That is a claim that the game conceals how outcomes are determined, which is the core of Illusion of Control. No monetary mechanic, schedule, social mechanic, or chance-based reward purchase is described; the whole review concerns one design behavior, the rigged dice.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "Dice rolls are not random at all, they are preplanned. Game randomly decides at the start of the play that which player has to win and dice rolls according to it. You also will know at the beginning of the game that who is going to win. One player will get more and more 6s and another one will get 1 or 2 mostly.",
      "rule_applied": "Illusion of Control boundary rule: 'Assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes, a non-random deal, a fixed win ratio, engineered near-misses...'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts the dice outcomes are secretly predetermined by the game to fix the winner, i.e., the game manipulates the determination of results rather than running a fair random process."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing two separate experiences with the game. First, enjoyment of the characters and moves, which is ordinary positive evaluation and carries no dark pattern. Second, the downloading system: the reviewer says they thought the file was already downloaded but the game still performs an additional download once started. That describes the game's storage footprint being revealed only after the initial install, i.e., a fragmented download design. Third, the reviewer reports that matchmaking stops working after the additional download and attributes the fault to their wifi even though their wifi is fine; this is presented as a technical malfunction/bug, not a deliberate design behaviour, so under R11 it is not codable, and the mention of matchmaking as non-functional does not describe a competition mechanic that the game imposes on the player.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "Tech_FragmentedDownloads",
      "span": "the downloading system sucks I thought I already download the file but I still get an additional download",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: designs that obscure the true storage requirements of the game until the game starts; indicator 'Missing animations / assets with in-place downloading prompts when packages are declined' and 'Download wall' family",
      "rationale": "The span shows the game running an additional mandatory download after the player believed the file was fully downloaded, meaning the true storage requirement is only revealed inside the app after the initial install."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two distinct gameplay experiences. First, the game presents advertisements at regular intervals tied to match events — after every over and after every wicket — which the reviewer presents as forced consumption that wastes data and time. Second, the catch mechanic is described as asymmetrically resolved: the opponent's fielders catch easily while the player's team drops virtually every catch, implying the game's catch outcomes are skewed by design rather than by player timing or choice. The review is short and complains about both the ad cadence and the apparently rigged catch determination.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Too much ads are shown after every over and every wicket fell",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: 'Compelled to view advertisements'",
      "rationale": "The span states that ads are displayed to the player on a game-determined schedule at match events (every over, every wicket), meaning the player is exposed to advertisements they did not request during play."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "Opponent takes catches very easily while our team drops all the catches except one or two",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule: 'Assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes ... difficulty tuned to a hidden schedule'",
      "rationale": "The span asserts a systematic asymmetry in catch resolution favoring the opponent over the player's own team, implying the game script or difficulty tuning determines catch outcomes rather than reflecting the player's actions, without revealing that mechanism."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing two things about the hero unlock system. First, progression to new heroes is extremely slow: after roughly 100 fights they still only have the two starting heroes, which implies unlocking is tied to repeating matches many times over a long grind before any progress shows. The repeated fighting required to advance is the mechanic being described. Second, the in-game 'share to unlock this hero' reward feature does not work for the reviewer — that is a malfunction report about a social-sharing reward, not a description of an incentive actually operating, so R11 excludes it. The closing line ('thinking about installing' the app, i.e., uninstalling) expresses disengagement and is not a mechanic. No purchase, schedule, timer, or social obligation is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "takes quite a long time to unlock new heros...I have done close to 100 fights and still have the first two heros you get when you start the game",
      "rule_applied": "T_Grinding definition (repetitive tasks to make progress) and boundary rule: assign where the span describes the activity required for progress as repeated — here, repeated fights",
      "rationale": "The player must run the same repeated activity (fighting, ~100 matches) to unlock heroes, and the span shows that repetition yielding no visible progress, which is grinding."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a content-release bottleneck that has reached a level cap: the game has run out of new levels for them at around the 11,000th level. Their account of the last few weeks establishes a repeating schedule on the game's clock: the Champions League event runs for about two weeks, then new levels arrive, then the next Champions League begins almost immediately, and then there are no levels again for another two weeks. The complaint That levels exist for the Champions League while no ordinary levels are available expresses that this event cycle is the only thing to do while the game's next content drop is pending. The phrases 'get a champions league' and 'another champions league' name the event but not what it is, so a search is needed to identify it. From my understanding, the review is about forced waiting for progress due to game-determined content releases and about the Champions League event filling that gap; it is not about grinding, payment, or social mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "No new levels for 2 weeks and get a champions league. ... Get new levels on 3/2/26 only to go maybe 50 levels and no more levels. Come to 3/3, another champions league & have to wait another 2 weeks for levels.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator under Playing by Appointment, boundary rule vs Non-dark scheduling: progress is gated on the game's content-release schedule.",
      "rationale": "The span describes the game forcing the player to wait on its schedule for roughly two weeks because the game has no new levels to offer, with the Champions League event filling the gap; because the appointment is required for any progress, the wait is dark and plays by appointment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Toon Blast Champions League event what is it",
  "search_result": "Champions League is a competitive event in Toon Blast for players who completed all available levels, running in league cycles over roughly two-week periods while new level releases are pending."
}

