raw:
{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing how Homescapes pressures them after losing a puzzle level. Three related mechanics appear. First, losing triggers a message tying the loss to the leaderboard and to losing accumulated streak rewards, with payment offered as the way to avoid both: this is a monetized competitive prompt. Second, the leaderboard itself is a system that ranks the player against other players, which the reviewer explicitly resents being forced into ('force you to be competitive', 'opt out of the leaderboard system'). Third, the same post-loss reminder works as anticipatory pressure: the player is warned they will fall down the rankings and forfeit their streak rewards unless they act, which is designed to make them feel they are falling behind. The 'winning streak rewards' here are forfeited on losing a level, not on missing a daily login, so Daily Rewards does not apply; no schedule or timer is described, so Playing by Appointment does not apply either.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "every time you lose the game reminds you that unless you pay up, you're going to go down on the leaderboard and will lose all your winning streak rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win low-level indicator (Monetized Rivalries): exploiting player competitiveness to encourage spending to achieve high placement on a leaderboard",
      "rationale": "The span shows the game prompting the player, after every loss, to pay in order to keep their leaderboard position and streak rewards, i.e., money buys competitive standing."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "I hate how the game tries to force you to be competitive",
      "rule_applied": "Definition / Alt. def.: 'The game makes you compete against other players.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes being made to compete through the leaderboard system and being penalised for losing, i.e., head-to-head ranking against other players."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "unless you pay up, you're going to go down on the leaderboard and will lose all your winning streak rewards. it makes you feel bad for not winning every level on the first attempt",
      "rule_applied": "Alt. def.: 'If you stop playing you'll miss out on something or be left behind and unable to catch up.'",
      "rationale": "The reminder instills anticipatory pressure: the player is made to feel bad and anxious about falling behind other players and forfeiting rewards, pushing them to keep winning or pay to avoid the loss."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review makes two distinct complaints. First, the game was promoted as ad-free but shows ads anyway — that claim concerns marketing material outside the app, which R13 places outside the unit. Second, and within the installed game, the reviewer describes how an in-game ad behaves: the only way to get out of the ad is to go into the Play Store, which consumes more data. That is a dismissal mechanism that prevents the player from simply closing the ad, forcing continued engagement with the ad and routing the player through a store page to escape it. The exit-route design is the actual mechanic described and it is present in the game as played, so the disapproval of it (R7) does not suppress coding. The presence of ads alone would not be enough under the T_Advertisement counterexample, but the described dismissal obstacle relates the ads to being compelled to deal with them, which satisfies the definition.",
