raw:
{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes three grievances. First, daily missions cannot be completed within the number of attempts the game grants. Second, new chances to play regenerate on a timer of roughly two minutes each, which means play is gated by an in-game wait between sessions. Third, the items the game generates do not line up with what the orders require, a complaint about the game's item economy or output randomness. The second mechanic is the clearest dark pattern: an energy/chance system that refills on a real-time schedule forces the player to wait for the game's clock. The daily-missions complaint is anchored in that same attempt limit, but nothing describes a daily login reward calendar, a streak, or a penalty for missing a day, so it does not lift to Daily Rewards on its own. The order/item mismatch describes an outcome of the merge system without naming any manipulative design behind it, so it codes nothing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it takes nearly 2 minutes for new chances to play",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: 'Patterns that impose arbitrary wait times on players through in-game timers.'",
      "rationale": "New chances to play regenerate only after a timer expires, so the player must wait on the game's schedule rather than playing when they want, which is the canonical Wait to Play pattern."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is playing a casino-style slots app and describing the odds of the game itself. The account has three parts: the game is fun, the game is impossible to win, and the developer is trying to make money. The complaint that the developers 'make these things so fun to play but impossible to ever win' attributes the outcome distribution to deliberate design: winning is asserted to be impossible by construction, not merely unlucky on this player's session. That is a claim that the game determines or conceals the determination of its outcomes. The mention of 'spend more money' is hypothetical and framed as a reason not to return, so no purchase mechanic currently operating is described, and no wager or currency purchase is named, so Gambling and Pay to Progress are not supported. No wait, schedule, or grind is described either.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "I dont understand why you guys make these things so fun to play but impossible to ever win",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule under Illusion of Control: assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states the developers built the game so that winning is impossible, which asserts the game itself fixes the outcome of play rather than reporting a mere unlucky run."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review lists five separate grievances about the game. (1) The shooting timer being too short is a gameplay rule about turn length, not a manipulative scheduling mechanic. (2) The foul rule removing two chips instead of one describes an in-game penalty rule the reviewer finds unfair; nothing in the codebook covers a foul penalty of this kind. (3) Chest opening times of 8–12 hours describe an in-game timer that imposes an arbitrary wait on the player before a reward can be collected: that matches the Wait to Play pattern. (4) The Practice option disappearing when a network connection is detected is a quirky technical behavior with no listed dark-pattern equivalent. (5) Other players using cheats is player conduct, not a design that incentivizes dishonest play, so it does not trigger Encourages Anti-Social Behavior under its boundary rule.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "The openning time for the chests is too long, 1 or 2 hours is fine, but 8 and 12 hours?!...come on!",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: 'In-game timers that make you arbitrarily wait for something.'",
      "rationale": "Chests can only be opened after a fixed in-game timer of 8–12 hours, forcing the player to wait on the game's schedule to collect the rewards they have already earned."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is a complaint about monetization and competitive structure in War Robots. The mechanics it actually names are: (1) heavy advertisement volume ('bombarded with tons of advertisements'), which is stated only as bombardment with no claim of being forced to watch or rewarded for watching, so it does not instantiate Advertisement; (2) PvP matchmaking that constantly pairs the reviewer against opponents far above their level ('gamers way above your paygrade'), which the reviewer explicitly interprets as a deliberate design strategy to pressure spending ('The strategy is to make you spend and spend to reach to upper levels'); and (3) an escalating price claim ('The more you pay, more the prices start to increase as well') that fits no listed label. The core coded content is competitive play against superior opponents whose advantage is tied to paying, plus the reviewer's assertion that matchmaking is deliberately arranged to extract money for advancement.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "you are always competing with gamers way above your paygrade",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes being placed in competition against other, higher-ranked players, which is the game making players compete against each other."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "The strategy is to make you spend and spend to reach to upper levels",
      "rule_applied": "Monetized Rivalries / Pay Wall indicators under Pay to Progress",
      "rationale": "The span asserts that the design pairs the player with stronger opponents and that reaching upper levels is achieved through repeated spending, i.e., exploiting competitiveness to make the player pay to progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "you notice you are always competing with gamers way above your paygrade. The strategy is to make you spend and spend to reach to upper levels.",
      "rule_applied": "boundary rule: assign where the span asserts matchmaking is manipulated or tuned to a hidden purpose",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts that matchmaking deliberately pits them against far stronger players as a strategy to induce further spending, a claim of manipulated matchmaking serving the developer rather than genuine competition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}
